# Normalize to LF in the repository. Studio is built on Windows (Lazarus/Delphi) and its
# CI runs on Linux; Pascal sources travel between the two, and a CRLF round-trip through
# git would show every line as changed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on a Windows checkout: CRLF breaks the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Pascal sources
*.pas text eol=lf
*.lpr text eol=lf
*.lfm text eol=lf
*.inc text eol=lf

# Prompt fixtures. These are compared BYTE FOR BYTE against src/SpxPrompt.pas, and the builder
# they were taken from joins every block with '\n'. The global rule above already gives LF, but
# it is spelled out here because a normalisation applied to these files would not look like a
# checkout setting -- it would look like a broken port, on a machine where the port is fine.
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.system.txt   text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.user.txt     text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.brief.txt    text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.template.txt text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.tsv          text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/*.json         text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/VERSION        text eol=lf

# And the clean-model-output cases get NO normalisation at all, because one of them is a CRLF
# fence and that is the whole case. The rule above was written to protect these files and, on
# its first commit, silently rewrote `clean-fence-crlf.in.txt` from CRLF to LF -- git said so in
# one warning line, and the suite stayed green because the working tree still held the original.
# A fresh clone would have got a case that tests nothing while claiming to test CRLF, which is
# worse than a failure. These are machine-generated and machine-compared; read them through
# scripts/dump-prompt-fixtures.mjs, not through a diff.
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/clean-*.in.txt  -text
tests/fixtures/prompt-v2/clean-*.out.txt -text

# Binary assets, never diffed or line-normalized
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.res binary
